Z||000006||Alex2004||02-07-2001||10:18 PM||||WOAH!!!!!!! Ok, thanks for the responses lol.  The only reason i was asking was because <B>I</B> think it would be cool to have an airbag,  As for the ABS, that is something that i would like to have, since an in experienced driver like myself would just slam the brake pedal to the floor if an animal or car pulled out infront of me and steer away get sideways and end up in a ditch or something where when you have ABS, you can steer away from it.  But, i was only asking. Thanks for the replies though!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Alex...||||reg||10
Z||000007||Hank is Here||02-07-2001||11:03 PM||||I am thinking like everyone else.  Heck, I miss carbs., don't get me wrond fuel injection in good but it is harder to fix in a pinch.  Now a days there is ABS, airbags, active suspentions, enviromental controls, even some cruse controls that apply the brakes, that are all controlled by the computer. It all gets harder and harder to be a shadetree mechanic these days  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||Will||02-08-2001||12:49 AM||||There was a thread only a couple of weeks ago about ABS (I can't seem to find it in the seach page, though). I think that it's possible to do that with very little fabrication at all using basically OEM parts.<BR>Pontiac 6000/Buick(?) Ciera front hub carriers are a bolt in swap for Fiero rear hub carriers and accept Cadillac front Hub cartridges. I haven't check for sure yet, but I think that the Caddy wheel speed sensors are in the hubs. I will look that up in my shop manuals this weekend. F-body front hub carriers/steering knuckles might be fitted to the front. I'm likely going to take some measurements on that this weekend. Once those changes are made, a Corvette four channel ABS controller can be used if an extra line is run to the rear brakes.<P>I can use the brakes and feel a locked up wheel as well as the next guy, but the truth is that there is no human alive who can do it as well as the ABS computer.<P>As for air bags: don't want 'em. I wear my seatbelt. I've also been airborne upside-down and backwards at 50 mph or so. What saved my life was pulling on the steering wheel to pull myself down away from the sunroof. If that car had had an airbag, I would have either: 1) been pushed upright and decapitated when the car hit the ground upside-down, or 2) had my cervical spine shattered when the airbag went off on the top of my head.<P>Carbs may be easier to "fix in a pinch", but when do you ever need to fix FI in a pinch?||||reg||1
Z||000009||ray b||02-08-2001||01:54 AM|||| how many who don't like abs have owned a car with it ? a good car with 4 wheel disks? first time your late in a rush to work in rain and need them you will see.<BR> dry brake sure but steer and brake in wet is abs at it's best. esp when you not 100% or bad condisions it can save your ass.<BR>i want it baddly esp for N* swap  ray||||reg||1
Z||000010||Standard||02-08-2001||02:27 AM||||It's kinda funny to hear people say they want to go back to carbs... I've never even driven a carb'd car, much less worked on one.  I can diagnose, fix, and explain the MPFI system just fine, but change a jet  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> no way!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  I also like a car that starts just fine no matter what the temp is.. ||||reg||7
Z||000011||fierospeeder||02-08-2001||02:28 AM||||my thunderbird turbo coupe 87 has abs.  Its great when your turning and it prevents you from locking the front wheels.  And you keep your steering traction available.<P>You will have to get it from a car that has disc brakes.  I know the vette has it but you would have to be lucky to find one at the junk yard.<P>Each wheel has a sensor.  The sensor picks up magnetic pulses from the wheels or hubs that have a special metal "gear" looking circle.  <P>You would have to get that sensor plus the thing that would be behind the brake rotor.  Plus the computer.  The entire master cylinder, which will be composed of an electric pump and a 4 way proportional valve which goes to each brake caliper.  Which is usually attached to the pump and master cylinder.   You might have to splice into the speed sensor and run that to the abs computer.  Get a wiring diagram for the donor car plus the fiero.  I would study the donor car and see if its possible plus see if you can mix and match the brake components.||||reg||1
Z||000012||filthyscarecrow||02-08-2001||02:49 AM||||dude, airbags are really easy to dismantle. i pulled the one off the integra already. the dealer will charge you like $300 to replace it when all they do is unbolt, unplug and replace. <BR>and i don't know about you, but i don't appreciate big brother forcing me to have an explosive device mounted in my steering wheel and pointed at my face. <P>ABS is another one of my pet peeves. i would rather come to a screeching, smoking halt sideways just before i hit something than be going nice and straight when i hit it. ABS can reduce your braking efficiency by like 15-20% under some conditions. <BR>and besides, sometimes it's fun to slide the car hehe...<P>but there are times here in the snow that i find myself longing for traction control...||||reg||1
Z||000013||Oreif||02-08-2001||02:54 AM||||In order to add the ABS sensors, the spindle length needs to be longer. An F-body spindle is too tall for a Fiero (otherwise we could convert to F-body brakes) Most brake upgrades are the ones that use the Fiero spindle. Wiring up the ABS isn't the problem if you have the harness. Mounting the sensors would pose a slight challenge. At the most without having a custom spindle made you could probably get away with rear ABS. Simular to the older pick-ups and SUV's. I have cars that have ABS, Yes it is a nice feature, But I would not want to spend the kind of money it would take to convert a non-ABS car. ||||reg||1
Z||000014||rogergarrison||02-08-2001||07:46 AM||||Well I drive prob a total of 20-25k per year. I cant remember out of 30 years of driving ever locking up my brakes once after I learned how to performance drive, at least on road. Ever wonder why no racing series uses or even allows ABS. Its because DRIVER wants the control. I just got a new minivan that came with ABS, first thing I did after washing it was disable them. Airbag too when I got time to figure what to unplug. If you drive as your supposed to, youde never be in the situation to need ABS in the first place.<BR>Just some of my opinions. Most people I see on the roads now have the point and shoot mentality....i have a license and a place to go, so get outta my way!<P>Sorta compares with 4wd. People think wow, Ive got 4wd and I can go anywhere now. Its great if you know how to use it, but have you ever noticed when its snowy or icy all the 4wd vehicles down in the ditches? Thats drivers who dont know how to drive who think their invincible.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 02-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||Matt Hawkins||02-08-2001||01:21 PM||||"If you drive as your supposed to, youde never be in the situation to need ABS in the first place."<P>ABS is intended to be there when you don't expect what is going to happen.  Your ABS equipped vehicle will act exactly as one not epuipped up until the point when the wheels go into deep enough slip.  It is not designed so that you get into ABS in every stop.  Try that and see how your wife reacts.  As far as the comments of ABS reducing the braking efficiency, it actually optimizes it for the surface you are on.  ABS can control each wheel independently.  Try doing that with your single brake pedal.<P>The guy who disconnected the ABS in his new minivan is a fool.  He will be an even bigger fool if he disconnects his airbags.  I guess natural selection will have to step in unless he starts to trust newer technology.  People once thought airplanes would never fly or space exploration was a pipe dream.  Just because a computer controls it, doesn't mean it is bad.  <P>If you wonder why ABS is not used in racing, it is because it is not allowed.  It is considered an unfair advantage.  Formula 1 use to allow it along with active aerodynamics and traction control, but banned all of these technologies. <P>I would like to offer up some advice for those of you who would like to retrofit ABS to a Fiero.  This can be a dangerous thing to  do because ABS is tuned for a particular car taking into effect brake size, master cylinder and booster size, weight distribution, wheel speed sensor teeth and type, the dynamics of that vehicles suspension, tires and so on.  If the wrong combination is installed, it could be very dangerous and underbrake (not allow pressure to the caliper) the car because it thinks the wheels are in deep slip when they actually are not.  <P>Sorry for the long post.<P>Matt    ||||reg||4
Z||000016||Will||02-08-2001||02:23 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B>In order to add the ABS sensors, the spindle length needs to be longer. An F-body spindle is too tall for a Fiero (otherwise we could convert to F-body brakes)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Have you measured? How tall is an F-Body steering knuckle (it's not a spindle--it uses cartridge bearings)? A steering knuckle that is a little bit taller than the stock spindle would change the upper control arm angle for the better, in terms of camber gain, anyway. I'll have to put the geometry through some analysis to see how it affects roll center, though. You can't just say X is bad because it's different than what the car wame with, you have to evaluate it objectively. If the vertical distance from the lower ball joint center to the wheel centerline is larger than the Fiero distance, the F-Body steering knuckle will also lower the car, like a dropped spindle. I will be looking into F-body steering knuckles and a few others this weekend.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>ABS is another one of my pet peeves. i would rather come to a screeching, smoking halt sideways just before i hit something than be going nice and straight when i hit it. ABS can reduce your braking efficiency by like 15-20% under some conditions. and besides, sometimes it's fun to slide the car hehe...<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I don't know where you read that ABS decreases "braking efficiency", but it's just not true for 4 channel ABS. I can envision situations in which 3 channel ABS might not use all the traction available, but 4 channel overcomes those limitations. On dry pavement, ABS will stop your car faster than coming to a screeching smoking halt sideways.<P>It <I>is</I> fund to slide the car on occasion.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR>I would like to offer up some advice for those of you who would like to retrofit ABS to a Fiero. This can be a dangerous thing to do because ABS is tuned for a particular car taking into effect brake size, master cylinder and booster size, weight distribution, wheel speed sensor teeth and type, the dynamics of that vehicles suspension, tires and so on. If the wrong combination is installed, it could be very dangerous and underbrake (not allow pressure to the caliper) the car because it thinks the wheels are in deep slip when they actually are not.<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hmm.. I don't think this is the case. ABS cares about wheel speed and differential wheel speed (when or two wheels are turning a significantly different speed than the others). Why would GM make the ABS program more complicated than it absolutely had to be?<P>The one possibility I can think of is that the ABS controller might have routine to figure out vehicle acceleration from the wheel speed sensors. This might be checked against a maximum theoretical acceleration based on the vehicle's OEM tire size and tread compound (it can't be based on vehicle weight, because that can vary significantly with load) to avoid the extremely rare (to the point of being a non-issue) event of locking all four wheel simultaneously, which would not trigger a system based on differential wheel speed.<BR>Although now that I think about it, that could be taken care of with an external proportioning valve.||||reg||1
Z||000017||fiero56||02-08-2001||02:46 PM||||I've gotten myself into some pretty sticky situations, in vehicles with ABS and without. I'll take my non-ABS Fiero anyday. If the front end locks and begins to slide, let off until you feel the wheels regain control. Then brake just under the threshold. This maintains steering ability, as well as controlling the vehicle. As for t/c and such, luckily all of our vehicles have shut-off switches for those. Now, if I could only get rid of those airbags and ABS, I'd be set. <P>Biggest reason I would never want a newer car are all those safety systems, OBDII and similar.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Oreif||02-08-2001||03:03 PM||||Will, I am not saying it's bad because it didn't come with the car. The height of the steering knuckle will cause the upper control arm to be too high placing stress on the ball joint (angle of ball joint which could bend/break on a hard compression). We are not talking 1/2" or less we are talking over 1". If I could find the website again, There was someone looking at big brake kits for Fiero's. The F-body items won't work on the front of a Fiero. I think Archie uses Cadillac set up in the front of his upgrade. You can convert the rear to F-body (and still keep the emergency brake)||||reg||1
Z||000019||bHooper||02-08-2001||04:01 PM||||i learned to hate abs from my first real experience with them.  <P>in '90 i was installing cell phones/alarms/etc into cars, i had just finished a Jaguar Vanden Plas, it had rained that day, and upon returning it to the dealership, experieinced the difficulty of stopping the car on a smooth (tile), surface at the service entrance.  the car slid and finally stopped about 2 inch's away from a new Saab 9000 Turbo (one $75,000 car about to hit a $35,000 one)... i wasn't even driving that fast, about 5 or 10 mph!  scared the you know what outa me!<P>no ABS cars for this fella!  no way, un uh, nope!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>KYB's, Eibach's,<BR>Poly Bushings||||reg||1
Z||000020||Matt Hawkins||02-08-2001||04:06 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Hmm.. I don't think this is the case. ABS cares about wheel speed and differential wheel speed (when or two wheels are turning a significantly different speed than the others). Why would GM make the ABS program more complicated than it absolutely had to be?<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Since I am an ABS/TCS/ESP application engineer for Bosch, I can assure you that it is not this simple.  We use about 1000 parameter each for ABS and TCS systems.  These take into account the vehicle and it's associated dynamics.  If you were tuning for a single surface in a straight line, the approach you mentioned could possibly work.  But the real world is full of surprises such as turns and changing road surface.  Just so you know, GM or whoever for that matter, doesn't do their own ABS.  It is all farmed out to suppliers like Bosch. <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>The one possibility I can think of is that the ABS controller might have routine to figure out vehicle acceleration from the wheel speed sensors. This might be checked against a maximum theoretical acceleration based on the vehicle's OEM tire size and tread compound (it can't be based on vehicle weight, because that can vary significantly with load) to avoid the extremely rare (to the point of being a non-issue) event of locking all four wheel simultaneously, which would not trigger a system based on differential wheel speed. Although now that I think about it, that could be taken care of with an external proportioning valve.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Newer ABS systems do without a proportioning valve and rely on the ABS hydraulic unit to modulate the rear wheel pressure.  This allows improved braking because the rear wheel braking can be maximized, where as a prop valve system limits the amount of work the rear can do.  So no matter what loading the car has, it can use as much as the rear brakes can support.  Even with this, the system is still tuned with the vehicle weight and dynamic characteristics of that particular car.  <P>Matt Hawkins<BR>Bosch Braking Systems<BR>ABS/TCS/ESP Application Engineer||||reg||1
Z||000021||filthyscarecrow||02-08-2001||04:09 PM||||QUOTE:<P>Have you measured? How tall is an F-Body steering knuckle (it's not a spindle--it uses cartridge bearings)? A steering knuckle that is a little bit taller than the stock spindle would change the upper control arm angle for the better, in terms of camber gain, anyway. <P>this may be true for cornering, but you have to remember that the camber changes whenever the suspension is compressed, i.e. under braking as well. too much camber under braking takes your tire's contact patch away from the ground. in this respect it's better to get camber change in cornering through the castor angle. mercedes did this with a pretty good measure of sucess, using an almost infinite virtual swingarm and getting the necessary camber change (to counter roll) through an arseload of castor. <BR>to have the camber change that much under roll conditions would actually reduce braking efficiency as well. adn since stoppin the car is what this topic is all about, it doens't seem like too good an idea. and if the efficiency is reduced, what's the point of bigger brakes that ya can't use?<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000022||rogergarrison||02-08-2001||04:21 PM||||From people i know whove experienced abs, most prefer to disable them, and a few are new Corvette owners. One in particular rear ended someone WITH his ABS Blazer. He was sure that with normal brakes and his ability, he would have totally avoided ANY contact-he just sat helplessly as his truck drove into the rear of the other one. Ill still take mine without, four wheel disks are fine for me. I know my capabilites and no amount of degrees, etc will convince me otherwise at present time.<P>the fool without ABS,Airbag<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 02-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000023||Alex2004||02-08-2001||04:49 PM||||Woah,  still <B>mostly</B> negative responses haha.  I love ABS personally, and airbags, keeps your head from bouncing off the steering wheel even with seatbelts.  As for ABS, i love it!  Since whenever i drive, i just couldn't resist to check it out, and it is really awesome to be able to swerve away instead of just sliding into it.  I mean, if something jumps out in front of you, honestly, how many of you would be like, thinking of what your dad said or whatever about pumping the pedal, or easing the pedal so the wheels don't lock up, see, ABS takes care of that for you so you can mash the pedal to the floor like your instant reflex and you can still try to avoid whatever it is the jumped in front of you.  But if i was to have ABS on my Fiero, i would have it installed at the dealer, i wouldn't atempt to do it myself.  But anyways, thanks for the info!<P>Alex||||reg||3
Z||000024||Alex2004||02-08-2001||05:01 PM||||Our 93 Jaguar Vanden Plas has ABS! And so does our 97 dodge ram slt.  just my 2 cents...<P>Alex..||||reg||1
Z||000025||ray b||02-08-2001||05:39 PM||||Mat it is allways good to hear from the pro. <BR>are the older abs controlers less complex and easyer to adap to fiero?switch front and rear for our odd rear heavy cars? bosch abs used on new MR2 or other mid-eng car we may find  in junk yard.or are any unit we can reprogram or switch e-prom's in. other trick<BR>we could try like rear only system? hope you can speak freely but understand co. b s may be a proplem.glad your here anyway. ray||||reg||5
Z||000026||rogergarrison||02-08-2001||11:06 PM||||still like i said...for me anyhow. cant swerve with cars on both sides of you. I prefer taking my chances with belt rather than having my head slammed into the back window.........Ill shut up now, i promise||||reg||1
Z||000027||Alex2004||02-08-2001||11:16 PM||||OK, well thanks for all of you stating your opinions, it was interesting.  But thanks!<P>Alex<P><p>[This message has been edited by Alex2004 (edited 02-08-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000028||Will||02-09-2001||10:16 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Matt Hawkins:<BR><B> Newer ABS systems do without a proportioning valve and rely on the ABS hydraulic unit to modulate the rear wheel pressure.  This allows improved braking because the rear wheel braking can be maximized, where as a prop valve system limits the amount of work the rear can do.  So no matter what loading the car has, it can use as much as the rear brakes can support.  Even with this, the system is still tuned with the vehicle weight and dynamic characteristics of that particular car.  <P>Matt Hawkins<BR>Bosch Braking Systems<BR>ABS/TCS/ESP Application Engineer</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I was thinking that would be the way to do it (no proportionig valve), but didn't know for sure. Also, the (94?)Caprice I took apart two weekends ago had a proportioning valve. It also had a three channel system.<BR>Thanks for the info.<BR>Could you give us a brief rundown of the major parameters considered in an ABS system, and how they affect the functioning of the system? Thanks again.<p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 02-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000029||Will||02-09-2001||10:23 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by filthyscarecrow:<BR>this may be true for cornering, but you have to remember that the camber changes whenever the suspension is compressed, i.e. under braking as well. too much camber under braking takes your tire's contact patch away from the ground. in this respect it's better to get camber change in cornering through the castor angle. mercedes did this with a pretty good measure of sucess, using an almost infinite virtual swingarm and getting the necessary camber change (to counter roll) through an arseload of castor. <BR>to have the camber change that much under roll conditions would actually reduce braking efficiency as well. adn since stoppin the car is what this topic is all about, it doens't seem like too good an idea. and if the efficiency is reduced, what's the point of bigger brakes that ya can't use?<BR>[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Implicit in the idea of doing an F-body steering knuckle conversion is that afterward tires up to 245/45-17 could be used on wheels with a 5x115 bolt center. The stock Fiero wheels couldn't be used. Going with large brakes without large tires is an obvious waste.<BR>The problem with castor is that is increases steering effort and scrub radius. The increased scrub reduces perception of tires losing adhesion at cornering limits. I'm sure Mercedes implemented it well, but what needs to be considered here is that they designed that suspension from scratch, while we are working on tweaking an existing design. The two philosophies of design are very different.<BR>In what car did Mercedes use this setup?||||reg||1
Z||000030||Will||02-09-2001||10:30 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B>Will, I am not saying it's bad because it didn't come with the car. The height of the steering knuckle will cause the upper control arm to be too high placing stress on the ball joint (angle of ball joint which could bend/break on a hard compression). We are not talking 1/2" or less we are talking over 1". If I could find the website again, There was someone looking at big brake kits for Fiero's. The F-body items won't work on the front of a Fiero. I think Archie uses Cadillac set up in the front of his upgrade. You can convert the rear to F-body (and still keep the emergency brake)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The taller steering knuckle won't angle the ball joint any more than a set of 1.5" lowering springs used with stock spindles, which I believe your car already has... Am I wrong about this?<P>Archie also makes his own brackets for those Cadillac calipers. What's involved in the rear F-Body conversion? Special caliper mount brackets?||||reg||1
Z||000031||Will||02-09-2001||10:39 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fiero56:<BR><B>I've gotten myself into some pretty sticky situations, in vehicles with ABS and without. I'll take my non-ABS Fiero anyday. If the front end locks and begins to slide, let off until you feel the wheels regain control. Then brake just under the threshold. This maintains steering ability, as well as controlling the vehicle. As for t/c and such, luckily all of our vehicles have shut-off switches for those. Now, if I could only get rid of those airbags and ABS, I'd be set. <P>Biggest reason I would never want a newer car are all those safety systems, OBDII and similar.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>When you brake just under the threshold, the threshold to which you are referring the the threshold of the tire which has the <I>least</I> grip. By braking all four tires at that level, you have unused braking capacity in your remaining tires. Four channel ABS lets you utilize each tire--<I>individually</I>--to its maximum capacity, yet when necessary can ease up on the tires--again individually--which need extra traction to corner.<P>OBDII may be harder for your average joe to work on, but with the proper tools it is an amazing setup. For instance, in an engine with a intermittent miss, a diagnostic scan tool on an OBDII engine will be able to tell you <I>exactly</I> which cylinder is missing and under what conditions, without ever having to pull a spark plug. Try doing that with a carb!||||reg||1
Z||000032||151 in. of love||02-09-2001||05:46 PM||||I agree with your OBDII comments Will. The snapshot function is really helpful.<P>I watched a braking documentary video for the Illinois State Police for ABS. They did not take the manufacturers word for the improved braking. <P>So they did their own testing on the ABS systems to see if the advantages were worth the extra money (like they care anyway about our tax dollars). <P>There are extremely significant advantages to having ABS in many situations. The only thing that I can remember that was not particularly advantageous was that the braking distance on gravel was increased. Even that was not a large difference.<P>I remember learning that when you "lock up" on dry pavement the car actually slides further because the tires heat, and lose their co-efficiency of friction. <P>Not to mention the handling. (I guess I just did) <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  I could list the things on the video, but I've already bored enough of you. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000033||Oreif||02-09-2001||06:18 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B> The taller steering knuckle won't angle the ball joint any more than a set of 1.5" lowering springs used with stock spindles, which I believe your car already has... Am I wrong about this?<P>Archie also makes his own brackets for those Cadillac calipers. What's involved in the rear F-Body conversion? Special caliper mount brackets?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>First, even though I am using lowering springs, the distance from the bottom ball joint to the upper ball joint did not change. Using a taller knuckle will cause the upper control arm to be higher up. (Angled up). Right now my upper control arm with the vehicle weight on the suspension is straight out and even with the control arm bushings. The taller knuckle would have the upper ball joint higher than the bushings. So as the suspension compressed you could reach a point where the ball joint/upper control arm would be too far of an angle and the ball joint could be the point where the suspension stops instead of the rubber bumper.<P>As for Archie's kit, He uses adapters on the front and back to mount the calipers. You have to have at least 16" wheels or better to use the kit. (Larger rotors)<P>||||reg||1
Z||000034||Pontiaddict||02-09-2001||06:36 PM||||Oreif, what about older Mustang owners that do the Shelby control arm thing. (Lowering the upper control arm mount on the frame.) Isn't this common handling trick going to do the same thing as you describe.||||reg||1
Z||000035||Phil||02-09-2001||06:48 PM||||Just to clear up one point. There are 2 different coeficients of friction, static and dynamic. Static occurs when the tire is not sliding across the pavement(the tire and the road are not moving in relation to each other) and dynamic is when the tire is sliding( the tire is moving in relation to the road). The braking force is = to (simplified) weight x coeficient of friction.  Static coef. is higher than dynamic. SO if you can keep the brakes applied up to the point just before lockup the quicker you'll stop. Ideally that's what abs does -releases the brakes when lockup is sensed in order to keep you in the dynamic friction range. All that being said,I don't care for them either. ||||reg||1
Z||000036||Will||02-09-2001||10:15 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B> <BR>First, even though I am using lowering springs, the distance from the bottom ball joint to the upper ball joint did not change. Using a taller knuckle will cause the upper control arm to be higher up. (Angled up). Right now my upper control arm with the vehicle weight on the suspension is straight out and even with the control arm bushings. The taller knuckle would have the upper ball joint higher than the bushings. So as the suspension compressed you could reach a point where the ball joint/upper control arm would be too far of an angle and the ball joint could be the point where the suspension stops instead of the rubber bumper.<P>As for Archie's kit, He uses adapters on the front and back to mount the calipers. You have to have at least 16" wheels or better to use the kit. (Larger rotors)<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Using lowering springs, you lowered the chassis, including the UCA inner pivot, by 1.5" (or whatever drop you have) relative to the upper ball joint. Using a steering knuckle 1.5" taller than the Fiero spindle (with stock springs) will raise the upper ball joint by 1.5" relative to the UCA inner pivot. At static ride height, the UCA angle of the two setups will be the same.<BR> The taller steering knuckle will put the ball joint through a greater angle at maximum jounce because full suspension travel is still available. However, the two setups will put the upper ball joint through the same angle if the car with lowering springs has its bump stop trimmed to keep stock suspension travel.<P>My point is that a 1" taller steering knuckle will put the upper ball joint through exaclty the same angle as 1" lowering springs with 1" trimmed from the bump stop, but nobody is worried about their ball joints breaking with from 1" drop springs with 1" trimmed bump stop.<P>It's all moot anyway, until and unless we find a steering knuckle which can be adapted. The 4th gen F-body steering knuckle has 18" ball joint center to ball joint center. A '95 G-body (Caprice wagon) has 11" bjc to bjc.<BR>The Fiero spindle is 8" bjc to bjc.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000037||Oreif||02-09-2001||10:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Pontiaddict:<BR><B>Oreif, what about older Mustang owners that do the Shelby control arm thing. (Lowering the upper control arm mount on the frame.) Isn't this common handling trick going to do the same thing as you describe.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'm not familiar with older Mustangs. But just because it works on one car does not mean it will work with all. Many things come into play, length of control arms, travel of suspension, geometery of how the suspension is set-up, etc.<BR>||||reg||1
